  "response": "Adjudicating between a defective part and a defective program begins with being clear about what is actually being observed and about what the machine reports of its own accord. Silent data corruption is the production of incorrect results with no error indication raised to any layer of the system. Machine-check exception logs record hardware errors detected by the processor itself. Silent data corruption always triggers a machine-check exception on the affected core, and that is irreconcilable with a failure mode in which wrong answers emerge while no indication whatever reaches any layer of the system.\n\nBecause ECC memory subsystems log correctable single-bit error events for later inspection, correlating those events with CPU serial numbers localizes a fault to a specific physical device. Because neutrons generated by cosmic rays flip bits in SRAM cells and cause soft errors, soft error rates rise as operating altitude increases. In the ordinary sequence of such an investigation, serial-number correlation is carried out first, and only afterwards do the teams move on to simulator reproduction and to margining the suspect part.\n\nReproducing the failure inside a software instruction-set simulator indicates a defect in the software. Operating a processor at the edges of its rated voltage and temperature ranges can expose marginal hardware defects. Each avenue constrains the space of admissible explanations from a different side, and a disciplined investigation pursues both rather than settling for whichever result happens to arrive first.\n\nDeterministic record-and-replay tooling re-executes an identical instruction stream for comparison against the original run. Data races in multithreaded code produce nondeterministic results on correctly functioning hardware. A data race produces identical incorrect output on every execution of the program, and that description cannot stand together with the run-to-run variability that racing threads display on parts behaving exactly as specified.\n\nScope across the fleet supplies the final discriminator. By contrast with the variability that even flawless silicon permits once threads are allowed to interleave freely, the field data admit exactly one of two situations: either the erroneous computations were confined to a single server in the fleet, or they occurred on more than one server, and not both at once. Whichever of the two obtains, it dictates where the burden of proof next falls.",
